{
  "content": {
    "body": "Workflow pebbles are flow graphs: nodes + edges + subprocess links.\nA node is unit-like — label, status, priority, description, refs — and\nstatuses per node are the product, exactly like table fields.\n\n## Node semantics (the canon)\n\n- Exactly ONE 'start'. Terminals: 'end' (the sole terminal — every path\n  ends at one) and 'error' (a FAILURE outcome that flows into its OWN\n  dedicated end) — one error per DISTINCT failure (rejected, cancelled,\n  validation-failure). There is NO 'success' node: a successful path just\n  flows to a plain 'end'.\n- EDGE CARDINALITY (CLI-ENFORCED — a violating write is rejected):\n  start = no inputs, exactly one output · step / query / subprocess /\n  cache / storage = many inputs, ONE output · api / events / queue /\n  timer = many inputs, ONE output OR a SOURCE with no input (a trigger/\n  injector) · condition = many inputs, multiple outputs (one per answer) ·\n  parallel = many inputs, multiple outputs (all fire simultaneously) ·\n  loop = many inputs, exactly TWO outputs (the \"Cycle\" line into the body +\n  the continuation) · end = many inputs, NO outputs · error = ONE input,\n  ONE output (to its own end).\n  Branching belongs to condition; fan-out belongs to parallel; looping\n  belongs to loop — never to a step.\n- 'query' = a DATABASE transaction — a query / insert / update / delete.\n  Works like a process (many inputs, ONE output) but marks a DB\n  touch-point; its description names the table(s)/operation.\n- INFRA touch-points — each many-in, ONE out, chosen by WHAT is touched;\n  prefer the specific one over a generic step or api:\n  'events' = publish/subscribe a domain event (a SOURCE when the flow is\n  triggered by an event); 'queue' = enqueue/consume a message or job (a\n  SOURCE for a consumer); 'cache' = a cache read/write/invalidate;\n  'storage' = object/blob/file storage (S3, filesystem — distinct from a\n  DB query); 'timer' = a delay/timeout/cron/scheduled trigger (a SOURCE\n  for a cron-kicked flow). Their descriptions name the concrete resource +\n  operation.\n- 'loop' = a LOOP head. Many inputs, exactly TWO outputs: the \"Cycle\" line\n  (a fixed, non-editable label) into the loop body — whose path ALWAYS\n  returns to the loop — and the unlabeled continuation to the code AFTER\n  the loop. Model an iteration/while/for/each here; the body's last node\n  points back at the loop.\n- 'step' (process) = one actor doing one thing at one altitude. It can\n  represent a single process or a whole group of processes — the\n  altitude decides. Label = imperative verb-phrase, ≤6 words (\"Create\n  work order\", never \"Work order creation\"). Description is MANDATORY —\n  at high altitude it carries the collapsed detail.\n- EVERY node DESCRIPTION is written in TWO TIERS, in this order: (1) PLAIN\n  LANGUAGE first — 1-3 sentences any stakeholder (product owner, QA,\n  non-engineer) can read: what this node is and how it works, in plain\n  English; (2) TECHNICAL last, a SEPARATE final paragraph — file:line,\n  symbols/signatures, types, rules. A description states ONLY what the node\n  IS + its technical detail — NEVER an issue, bug or verdict; anything wrong\n  belongs in a FINDING, never in the description. Be DETAILED — no stubs; an\n  api node carries its FULL contract (direction, comm type, complete\n  payload shape + requirements).\n- 'condition' = a question; label ends with \"?\". EVERY outgoing edge\n  carries its answer in the edge's label field (yes/no/enum) — NOT in\n  sourceHandle (that is a physical handle id l/r/b; a non-handle value\n  there makes the edge vanish). Need more outcomes? Chain conditions or\n  dispatch via a step + subprocess.\n- 'subprocess' = embeds another workflow: the way a too-large flow is\n  decomposed. ALWAYS carries the subprocess ref to the child pebble —\n  the CLI REJECTS a subprocess node without its child link. Label\n  matches the child workflow's title. In the app, right-click a process\n  → \"Convert to sub-process\" creates the child workflow (seeded from\n  the node's label/description) and swaps the node, ref wired — use the\n  same pattern when extraction later deepens a topic step.\n- 'api' = an EXTERNAL API / SYSTEM — anything OUTSIDE this app's boundary:\n  another backend/service, a frontend, a queue/message bus, a third-party\n  or partner API, a webhook peer, an AI service, etc. Works like a process\n  (many inputs, ONE output). It marks a crossing of the boundary in EITHER\n  direction, and its description MUST name three things: the DIRECTION\n  (inbound = received / outbound = sent), the COMMUNICATION TYPE (REST/HTTP,\n  webhook, gRPC, GraphQL, queue/message-bus, WebSocket, …), and the DATA\n  (its structure + requirements — auth, headers, validation), ref-linking\n  the api/structure pebble.\n  - INBOUND (we receive): if the flow is TRIGGERED by data from an external\n    system (an HTTP/REST request we serve, a webhook we receive, a gRPC\n    call, a queue message we consume), put an api node at the entry that\n    details what was received — right AFTER the start node (start = our\n    entry point; api = the sender + the received payload), OR, for a\n    continuous injector with no start of its own, as a SOURCE (no incoming)\n    pointing at the listener node. api sources are additional entry roots\n    alongside the single start.\n  - OUTBOUND (we send): a call that leaves our boundary mid-process — an\n    HTTP client call to another service, publishing to a queue, notifying a\n    frontend, calling a partner/AI API — is an api node, never hidden\n    inside a plain step. If it's the terminal step (push out, then done) it\n    connects to an end.\n  Identify these crossings actively: a controller/handler at the entry is\n  usually inbound; an SDK/client/publish/dispatch-to-another-system call in\n  the body is usually outbound.\n- 'exception' = hand-off to global error handling, IN the main flow. An\n  exception check (condition) points to it and it flows to its OWN end.\n  It is an OUTCOME marker like error: DUPLICATED, never shared —\n  each covered region gets its OWN exception node + own end (in-degree 1),\n  and a main flow normally has SEVERAL. Use it — NOT an arrow back UP to a\n  reused error/rollback box, NOT one shared node many paths point at —\n  when the error is caught by a handler that wraps MANY actions.\n- 'global-exception' = the ROOT of a SEPARATE error-handling flow drawn\n  beside the main one. Like start it takes no input and has ONE output;\n  it is NOT wired into the main flow — it is its own little graph (e.g. a\n  condition on error type fanning out to error terminals). It is an\n  additional entry root alongside the single start. Same Siren icon as\n  exception; the label (\"Global\") tells them apart.\n- 'try' = the OPEN of a try/catch — a guarded block (Shield icon, circle).\n  MANY inputs; exactly TWO outputs (CLI-enforced): the guarded BODY (the try\n  block's statements as ordinary nodes, flowing down, whose path RETURNS to\n  the catch) and a SPINE straight to the matching 'catch'.\n- 'catch' = the CLOSE of a try/catch — it handles failure (ShieldAlert\n  icon, circle). Exactly TWO inputs (the try's spine + the body's last node)\n  and exactly TWO outputs (CLI-enforced): the EXCEPTION line, ALWAYS labeled\n  \"Exceptions\" (fixed — not editable/removable), which leads to the failure\n  handling; and the CONTINUATION line, with NO label, which leads to the code\n  AFTER the try/catch. A try pairs with exactly one catch (see the try/catch\n  section below). Use 'try'/'catch' whenever the code runs work under a\n  guarded error-handling block — in ANY language and whatever its keywords\n  (try/catch, try/except, begin/rescue, do/catch, try-with-resources,\n  defer+recover, a Result/err-returning guarded region, …). YOU identify the\n  construct from the code; the node model is the SAME for all of them — do\n  not pattern-match a keyword. 'exception'/'global-exception' are for error\n  handling that is NOT a guarded try/catch block (a middleware/framework\n  filter, or a shared handler the catch's Exceptions line hands off to).\n- 'parallel' = REAL concurrency only (queue dispatch, async jobs,\n  Promise.all / goroutines / thread fan-out, event fan-out with\n  independent consumers). It may have multiple output edges. The\n  description MUST name the join semantics: ALL-complete, ANY-complete,\n  or fire-and-forget. Two sequential calls that merely \"could\" be\n  concurrent = sequence.\n- Well-formed: every non-end node has ≥1 outgoing edge; nothing is\n  unreachable from a root (start / external source / global-exception);\n  refs[] on any node touching an api, model, or governed behavior.\n\n## Exception handling — inline error vs exception vs global-exception\n\nFLOW FORWARD. The ONLY legitimate backward/loop edge is a genuine PROCESS\nretry — a step/subprocess/parallel (or a condition deciding whether to\nretry) looping back to an EARLIER process because the code re-processes,\nloops, or goes back and forth. Everything OUTCOME- or terminal-bound moves\nFORWARD: success, error, exception, rollback/compensation and end never\ntake a backward edge and are NEVER reached by pointing back up to reuse a\nshared box. If tempted to draw an arrow up to an existing outcome, put a\nNEW one forward instead. Error handling then chooses by SCOPE:\n\n- ONE action's own failure (an auth check, an input validation, a single\n  guard) → keep it INLINE: the action → its own 'error' node → an end,\n  right there below the check. Do not centralize it.\n- A GUARDED try/catch you identify in the code (in ANY language — try/catch,\n  try/except, begin/rescue, do/catch, defer+recover, an err-return guarded\n  region, …) → model it with the dedicated 'try'/'catch' nodes (see\n  \"Modeling a try/catch\" below): the 'try' opens the guarded region, the\n  body flows down and returns to the 'catch', and the 'catch' has two\n  outputs — the \"Exceptions\" line to the failure handling and the unlabeled\n  continuation to the next code.\n- A shared/cross-cutting handler that is NOT a guarded try/catch block (a\n  middleware error filter, a framework exception handler) → an 'exception'\n  node (the hand-off marker) → its OWN end in the main flow, plus the shared\n  handler modeled ONCE as a 'global-exception' flow (its REAL processes —\n  rollback, log, notify, mark-failed — as EXPLICIT nodes, usually after a\n  condition on the error TYPE → the error terminals). An 'exception' node\n  ALWAYS requires a 'global-exception' flow to exist (CLI-enforced).\n  exception nodes are DUPLICATED like success/error (each region its own,\n  in-degree 1) — NEVER reuse one, point many regions at one, or route back\n  up to a distant one. When a 'catch''s \"Exceptions\" line delegates to\n  that shared handler, it too routes into the 'global-exception' flow.\n\nSAME FILE — the global-exception flow lives in the SAME workflow pebble as\nthe main flow (its nodes go in the same content.nodes array). It is NOT a\nseparate pebble, and NOT a subprocess. It is simply a set of nodes with no\nedges connecting it to the main flow, laid out to the RIGHT of the main\nflow (the layout engine keeps it clear of the main flow's branches). So:\nexception (in the main flow) marks \"this is handed off to the exception\nblock\"; global-exception (a separate flow in the same pebble, off to the\nside) holds the catch's real processes and defines HOW they run. Together\nthey replace the back-up-arrow anti-pattern for cross-cutting errors, while\nsingle-purpose validations stay inline with their own error → end.\n\n## Modeling a try/catch — the dedicated 'try' and 'catch' nodes\n\nThis applies to EVERY programming language. YOU identify the guarded\nerror-handling construct in the code yourself — try/catch, try/except (Python),\nbegin/rescue (Ruby), do/catch (Swift), try-with-resources (Java), defer+recover\n(Go), a Result/err-returning guarded region, whatever the language uses — do\nNOT pattern-match a keyword or assume one syntax. Once you recognize it, the\nnode model is the SAME for all of them: model it with the dedicated 'try'\nand 'catch' node types (circles), which make the guarded block explicit —\nwhere it opens, where it closes, what runs on failure, and where the flow\ncontinues. The shape is CLI-enforced, so a bad try/catch is rejected on\nwrite; in the app, a brace whose logic isn't met is outlined dashed-red.\n\n1. THE 'try' NODE '{' opens the guarded region. It has exactly TWO\n   outgoing lines:\n   • the BODY — the try block's statements as ordinary nodes flowing down\n     (steps, conditions, externals, subprocesses…); the body's last node\n     RETURNS to the catch.\n   • the SPINE — a line straight to the matching 'catch' (the bracket\n     edge). The 2nd line may ONLY target a catch.\n2. THE BODY is modeled in FULL — being inside a try NEVER excuses collapsing\n   it. Every guarded step is its own node; an inner guard that throws keeps\n   its OWN condition with both arms, and its throw arm → an 'error' node\n   whose label states the throw (\"<what> — error thrown\") → its own end (its\n   position inside the guarded region conveys the catch handles it; no edge\n   to the catch is drawn). Never merge inner guards away, never drop them.\n3. THE 'catch' NODE '}' closes the block and handles failure. It has\n   exactly TWO incoming (the try's spine + the body's return) and exactly\n   TWO outgoing:\n   • the EXCEPTION line — ALWAYS labeled \"Exceptions\" (fixed, not\n     editable/removable) → the FAILURE HANDLING. What it leads to depends on\n     whether the error is HANDLED here or ESCAPES (see \"Caught vs uncaught\"\n     below): a catch that HANDLES it locally (rollback, log, recover,\n     set-status, return a default) → those real processes as EXPLICIT nodes,\n     in order → 'end'; a catch that RE-THROWS or delegates to the app's\n     global handler → its local step(s), then an 'exception' hand-off node →\n     'end', which routes to the 'global-exception' flow (exception ⟹\n     global-exception). Never a single generic \"handle error\" box; model each\n     step the catch runs.\n   • the CONTINUATION line — with NO label (a label can't be added) → the\n     code AFTER the try/catch (the happy path continues here).\n4. LAYOUT (auto-arrange) draws it as a BRACKET, unique to try/catch: the try\n   and its catch sit on the same straight spine column; the guarded body is\n   an indented column flowing down BETWEEN them; the catch sits on the spine\n   one row below the body's footprint (the special try↔catch distance); the\n   continuation continues straight down the spine and the \"Exceptions\" line\n   peels right. All other arrangement is unchanged.\n\nSo a try/catch reads: try '{' → body (each inner guard its own condition,\nany throw → 'error' \"…error thrown\" → end) ⟶ back to catch '}', with the\ntry→catch spine drawn straight; then catch —\"Exceptions\"→ [rollback → log →\n…handling…] and catch —(no label)→ the next code. A try has exactly one\ncatch; a catch has exactly two ins and two outs, one line labeled\n\"Exceptions\" and one unlabeled.\n\n## Caught vs uncaught — WHEN the global-exception flow appears\n\nThe trigger for a 'global-exception' flow is the SCOPE of handling, mirroring\nthe runtime: is the error handled where it occurs, or does it ESCAPE?\n- CAUGHT & handled by a try/catch (BOUNDED): it stays in that catch's own\n  \"Exceptions\" flow — the local handling steps → 'end'. Self-contained; it\n  does NOT create a global-exception. A catch that fully recovers is the\n  common case here.\n- UNCAUGHT / ESCAPED → the 'global-exception' flow (the app's global /\n  framework / middleware handler). This is warranted when the error is NOT\n  bounded by a local catch:\n    · a 'throw' / error-return with NO enclosing try/catch;\n    · an error that propagates PAST all catches to a top-level handler;\n    · a catch that RE-THROWS or delegates to the shared handler.\n  Model the escape point as an 'exception' node (the hand-off) → its 'end';\n  it goes to the 'global-exception' flow, which MUST exist (CLI-enforced:\n  exception ⟹ global-exception). The global-exception flow holds the shared\n  handler's REAL processes as EXPLICIT nodes (rollback, log, notify,\n  mark-failed → usually a condition on error TYPE → the error terminals),\n  laid out beside the main flow.\nSo: bounded catch → local, ends, no global-exception. Escape/rethrow/uncaught\n→ 'exception' → 'global-exception'. An 'exception' node therefore appears\nONLY when something genuinely escapes to the global handler — never for a\ncatch that fully handles its own error. Do NOT force every catch to a\nglobal-exception, and do NOT invent a global-exception the code has no\nhandler for.\n\n## Completeness — capture EVERY branch and exit (all languages)\n\nCompleteness comes before elegance, and it is LANGUAGE-AGNOSTIC. Enumerate\nevery point where control DECIDES or LEAVES — in whatever forms the language\nuses — and account for EACH as a node/branch (or fold it into a described\nstep with a cited reason; never silently drop one):\n- every conditional: if / else-if / else, ternary, null/optional check,\n  guard clause, early return, continue, break;\n- every multi-way branch: switch / match / case — INCLUDING the default or\n  fallthrough arm;\n- every loop, and how it exits or retries;\n- every THROW / raise / reject / panic / error-return site;\n- every catch / except / rescue CLAUSE — a multi-clause handler (multi-catch,\n  chained except, several rescue, a type-switch on the error) is SEVERAL\n  handlers: model each clause's DISTINCT handling (different logging, notify\n  flags, rollback, rethrow, mapped status/response), never merged into one;\n- every terminal outcome.\nA step that can fail INTERNALLY need not grow a branch per possible throw —\nthe enclosing catch's exception-type condition covers those collectively —\nbut an EXPLICIT decision or exit visible in the code that you did not model\nis a gap. Before finishing, LIST the source's decision + exit points and\nconfirm each maps to the graph; a mismatch means you are not done. This holds\nfor every language and paradigm — recognize the construct, then model it.\n\n## Altitude = node GRANULARITY, never node COUNT\n\nThere is NO node cap and NO target count — none. Node count is an\nOUTPUT of the altitude you chose, never a goal. Capture EVERY process\nthat qualifies at that altitude. Two ABSOLUTE rules at all depths:\nnever MERGE two distinct processes into one node, and never DROP a\nprocess to shrink the graph. If two things do different work they are\ntwo nodes — proximity, smallness, or similarity never justify\ncollapse. Many nodes is fidelity, not a defect; developers rely on\nthis to see every detail.\n\nAltitude only sets WHAT QUALIFIES as a node:\n1. Choose the altitude that matches the ask (shallow / extract / deep,\n   defined below by their node-qualification test).\n2. Include EVERY unit that qualifies at that altitude — all of them.\n3. \"Too many nodes\"? There are exactly two sanctioned responses, and\n   both PRESERVE every process:\n   - RAISE ALTITUDE — re-run coarser (extract → shallow): the inclusion\n     test changes, so distinct processes survive re-bucketed into fewer,\n     larger-grained topics; none is lost.\n   - SUBPROCESS DECOMPOSITION — keep the altitude, move a cluster into a\n     linked child workflow (deep-extract): the process still exists, one\n     tier down.\n   FORBIDDEN: merging distinct nodes or dropping any to reduce count.\n   Readability comes from hierarchy (child workflows) + shallow-first\n   navigation — never from deletion.\n4. Parallel needs evidence in code (queues/async/fan-out). When unsure:\n   sequence + a note.\n\n## Termination invariants (CLI-ENFORCED — a violating write is rejected)\n\nA workflow spec MUST terminate every path AT AN END NODE. The CLI\nrejects on: no end node; any non-end node with no outgoing line\n(success/error included — they must reach an end); a success/error\npointing at anything other than an end; a condition with fewer than two\narms; a parallel with fewer than two branches; ANY disconnected node —\na non-root node with no incoming line (roots = start, external sources,\nglobal-exception handlers), or a non-end node with no outgoing line, or\na node unreachable from any root; plus the cardinality canon (one start,\nend sink-only, step/subprocess/external/exception/success/error one\noutput). Every node must be fully wired into the flow — no orphans. AND an\n'exception' node REQUIRES a 'global-exception' flow in the same pebble\n(the exception is a hand-off; its catch processes live under the global-\nexception) — an exception with no global-exception is rejected.\n- TRY/CATCH cardinality is enforced: a 'try' has exactly TWO outputs (one\n  SPINE to a 'catch', one BODY that RETURNS to that catch); a 'catch' has\n  exactly TWO inputs (that spine + the body's return) and exactly TWO\n  outputs — one line labeled \"Exceptions\" and one with NO label. A try whose\n  body doesn't reach its catch, or a catch missing either output or its\n  exact labels, is rejected.\n- \"end\" is the SOLE terminal (sink-only). success = a positive result\n  signal · error = a failure signal · exception = hand-off to global error\n  handling — these are OUTCOME MARKERS, each with one output into a\n  DEDICATED end node it does not share (1:1). The CLI rejects an end fed by\n  an outcome that has any other incoming edge.\n- success/error are EVIDENCE-BASED, never manufactured. Emit success\n  only where the code produces an explicit positive signal (2xx/201\n  response, Ok(...), resolve(...), an emitted \"succeeded\" event, a\n  true success-branch); emit error only where it emits/handles failure\n  (throw/raise, Err(...), 4xx/5xx, reject(...), a catch that exits).\n  These live anywhere, not just APIs. NO cited outcome token → use a\n  plain end: a function that just computes and RETURNS A VALUE ends at\n  a plain end with no marker. Never split a plain return into\n  success+error.\n- OUTCOMES ARE DUPLICATED, NEVER SHARED. Each success/error/exception node\n  has exactly ONE incoming line and ONE dedicated end — a private chain\n  (check → outcome → its own end). Even when two paths hit the SAME\n  outcome type (two \"Invalid\" errors, two \"201\" successes, two regions\n  handled by the same global handler), you DUPLICATE: a separate outcome\n  node + separate end for each. The CLI rejects an outcome node with more\n  than one incoming path.\n- PROCESS CONVERGENCE stays legal — many steps/condition-arms/parallel-\n  branches MAY point at one shared step/subprocess/parallel (that shows\n  where the flow goes), and plain step-paths MAY share one plain end (a\n  common return point). Only success/error are duplicated, never\n  converged.\n- ONE outcome per DISTINCT externally-observable result (different\n  status code / caller-visible contract / compensating action). Auth\n  denial is always its own. 403/404/422/500 = four error outcomes, each\n  with its OWN end.\n\n## The three extraction depths (workflow-specialized)\n\n- SHALLOW (/pebbles-shallow-extract): a node qualifies when it is a\n  TOP-LEVEL TOPIC — a distinct, nameable phase/responsibility a reader\n  would list answering \"what are the major things this does?\" and that\n  could stand alone as its own workflow. Internals (individual calls,\n  branches, queries) do NOT qualify — they belong to a topic, described\n  not decomposed. Capture EVERY top-level topic (no cap); two topics\n  stay separate whenever they answer different \"what does it do\"\n  questions — proximity/size never collapses them, because each is a\n  future entry point the user may promote to its own workflow and\n  extract/deep-extract. Still terminate every path.\n- EXTRACT (/pebbles-extract): ONE symbol, EXHAUSTIVE, single level — a\n  node qualifies when it is ANY discrete operation at that level: every\n  call, branch, loop, guard, error path, query, event. Capture ALL of\n  them (no cap); callees are FLATTENED into described steps (cite their\n  file:line), not opened. Model every catch/throw as its own terminal.\n  May run on top-level scope too — just heavier.\n- DEEP (/pebbles-deep-extract): identical node-qualification to extract\n  (capture everything, no cap); its ONLY addition is PROMOTION — a\n  qualifying callee (imported service/event/file with real internal\n  flow) becomes a subprocess linking its OWN child workflow, decided by\n  the PROMOTION TEST below. The ENVELOPE PATTERN (a controller opening a\n  transaction and delegating the core mutation to a domain service) is\n  the canonical case: the domain call is the subprocess, the API layer\n  stays high. Promoted children are reconciled (query existing → link;\n  create only if absent), never re-traced. Parent AND every child\n  independently satisfy the termination invariants; cycles link, never\n  re-extract. Heaviest of the three; also runs on top-level scope.\n\n## deep-extract PROMOTION TEST — subprocess vs plain step\n\nFor EACH non-utility callee a topic invokes (service, dispatched event/\nhandler, imported file/function, job, command), apply in order — first\nmatch wins:\n- STEP 0 — gate utilities: formatters, mappers, validators, DTOs,\n  constants, thin wrappers, pure helpers → KEEP as a described step,\n  never promote, whatever their size.\n- STEP 1 — count the callee's DISTINCT operations (branches, I/O calls,\n  dispatched events, sequential domain steps — NOT lines): < 6 → KEEP\n  as a step (this is the tiny-child cutoff — a callee that would graph\n  to ~2–5 nodes is too small to earn its own workflow; note its\n  operations in the step's description). ≥ 6 → continue.\n- STEP 2 — is it self-contained and NAMEABLE as a noun-phrase (\"Create\n  Work Order\", \"Fan-out OrderPlaced listeners\")? A long-but-linear\n  inline sequence with no conceptual boundary → KEEP as a step. Names\n  cleanly → PROMOTE.\n- STEP 3 — PROMOTE: give it its OWN child workflow, link from the parent\n  as ONE subprocess node; recurse the same test inside the child.\n\nThis bar is a property of the CALLEE, evaluated per callee — it NEVER\ncaps the parent. The parent keeps every discrete operation as its own\nnode (a 40-step parent stays 40 nodes); promotion only replaces a heavy\ncallee's inline expansion with one subprocess node.\n\nScenarios (pattern-match these):\n- controller → WorkOrderService::create() runs validation, persistence,\n  3 side-effects, dispatches an event (~8 ops, names cleanly) → PROMOTE.\n- controller → WorkOrder::create($data) direct model insert (1 op) →\n  KEEP as step.\n- dispatch(OrderPlaced) with 6 listeners doing real work (email,\n  inventory, ledger, webhook…) → PROMOTE the handler fan-out.\n- dispatch(UserLoggedIn) with 1 listener writing a timestamp (1–2 ops)\n  → KEEP as step.\n- import formatMoney() used once → utility, KEEP (Step 0).\n- import GeocodeAddress whose own flow is 3 nodes → real logic but < 6\n  ops → KEEP (note the ops inline).\n\n## Depth selection (recommendation, not mandate)\n\n- shallow-extract — RECOMMENDED starting point for a whole app/repo:\n  top-level topics (each a promotable phase) so you see the overall flow\n  first. Not required — you CAN point extract/deep at a whole workflow,\n  it's just heavier (they capture every discrete step, even trivial\n  ones).\n- extract — one topic fully mapped, single file/level, callees flattened\n  to steps. Use when the topic is self-contained or you don't yet care\n  about callee internals.\n- deep-extract — same completeness PLUS promoting qualifying callees to\n  linked child workflows. Use when a topic delegates real work to\n  services/events/imported flows you also want navigable as their own\n  graphs. Best after shallow has shown where the substance lives.\n- Rule of thumb: shallow to MAP, extract to DETAIL one topic, deep to\n  detail a topic AND its substantial sub-flows.\n\nANTI-PATTERNS (both forbidden): SUBPROCESS EXPLOSION — promoting every\nimport/callee, shredding one readable flow into dozens of 1–2 node\nstubs; and its opposite, COLLAPSING a genuine ≥6-op service into one\nvague \"does stuff\" step. Promote iff it clears the ≥6-op nameable-flow\nbar; otherwise inline it — never hand-wave a real sub-flow away.\n\n## Writing a workflow — command shape (write it ONCE, correctly)\n\nNode fields: id (any stable string), label, status, nodeType,\ndescription?, refs?. OMIT x/y — positions are auto-filled and you run\narrange afterward (never hand-place). Edge fields: id, source, target,\nlabel? (the arm ANSWER shown on the edge — \"yes\"/\"no\"/status code —\nwhere a condition's branch text goes) and sourceHandle? (the outlet id\n\"l\"/\"r\"/\"b\" ONLY — NEVER the answer; prefer label + omit sourceHandle).\nnodeType is one of:\nstart, end, step, query, condition, subprocess, parallel, loop, events,\nqueue, cache, storage, timer, api, error, exception,\nglobal-exception, try, catch.\n  · query = a DB transaction (many in, ONE out).\n  · events = publish/subscribe a domain event (many in, ONE out; may be a\n    SOURCE — an event listener with no start).\n  · queue = enqueue/consume a message/job (many in, ONE out; may be a\n    SOURCE — a queue consumer with no start).\n  · cache = a cache read/write/invalidate (many in, ONE out).\n  · storage = object/blob/file storage — S3, filesystem (many in, ONE out).\n  · timer = a delay/timeout/cron/scheduled trigger (ONE out; may be a\n    SOURCE — a scheduled kick with no start).\n  · api = an external API call / system (many in, one out; may be a\n    SOURCE with no start).\n  · loop = a loop head (many in, exactly TWO out: the fixed-label \"Cycle\"\n    line into the body that returns here, and the continuation out).\n  · error = a failure outcome that flows into its OWN dedicated end. (There\n    is no \"success\" node — a successful path just flows to a plain end.)\n  Pick the MOST SPECIFIC infra node for a boundary touch: a DB write is a\n  query, a cache get is cache, an object-store put is storage, an event\n  publish is events, a job enqueue is queue — not a generic step or api.\n  (log/metric/validate/auth/serialize stay INSIDE a node's description —\n  fan-in utilities are never their own node.)\n\ncreate (or update <path> --patch with a {\"content\": ...} wrapper) takes\nthis exact shape:\n\n  \"$PEBBLES_CLI\" create workflows --title \"Reset Password\" --content JSON\n  where JSON =\n  {\n    \"kind\": \"flow\",\n    \"nodes\": [\n      {\"id\":\"start\",\"label\":\"POST /reset\",\"status\":\"implemented\",\"nodeType\":\"start\",\"description\":\"…file:line\"},\n      {\"id\":\"valid\",\"label\":\"Token valid?\",\"status\":\"implemented\",\"nodeType\":\"condition\",\"description\":\"…\"},\n      {\"id\":\"err\",\"label\":\"422 Invalid\",\"status\":\"implemented\",\"nodeType\":\"error\",\"description\":\"…\"},\n      {\"id\":\"errEnd\",\"label\":\"End\",\"status\":\"implemented\",\"nodeType\":\"end\"},\n      {\"id\":\"reset\",\"label\":\"Update password\",\"status\":\"implemented\",\"nodeType\":\"query\",\"description\":\"…\"},\n      {\"id\":\"okEnd\",\"label\":\"200 OK — End\",\"status\":\"implemented\",\"nodeType\":\"end\"}\n    ],\n    \"edges\": [\n      {\"id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"start\",\"target\":\"valid\"},\n      {\"id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"valid\",\"target\":\"err\",\"label\":\"no\"},\n      {\"id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"valid\",\"target\":\"reset\",\"label\":\"yes\"},\n      {\"id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"err\",\"target\":\"errEnd\"},\n      {\"id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"reset\",\"target\":\"okEnd\"}\n    ]\n  }\nThen run: \"$PEBBLES_CLI\" arrange workflows/reset-password.pebble\nGet the shape right in ONE write — every field above is required except\nx/y (omit), description (recommended), refs, and sourceHandle.\n\n## Post-commit side effects\n\nModel the transaction as Begin → … → Commit with an explicit Rollback\npath. A side effect that can fail AFTER commit (event dispatch, outbox,\nnotification) gets its own error/compensation arm or an in-transaction\noutbox step with a cited at-least-once guarantee — never a silent\nfire-and-forget.\n\nALL depths obey the extraction-scope rule: Scope Block first (entry\npoint as file:symbol, confirmed when ambiguous), forward-closure\nrelevance only (upstream/siblings OUT), utility fan-in filter, every\nnode cites an in-closure location, exclusion list emitted, branch\ncounts reconciled.\n\n## Verification (node by node)\n\n- Per-node statuses: implemented = code exists at the ref/codeLink and\n  matches the described happy path · verified = matched AND covered by\n  a passing test you LOCATED (name it in the finding) · drift = code\n  diverges from the description · missing = no code found. Never\n  verified without a named test.\n- Conditions: verify EVERY arm — locate the predicate, trace each\n  outlet to its target. An untraced arm ⇒ that arm's TARGET gets\n  drift/missing; the condition itself can stay implemented.\n- Parallel: verify each branch independently, then the JOIN — the code\n  must actually await all branches (or match the documented ANY /\n  fire-and-forget). A missing join = ONE finding on the parallel node.\n- Findings: one per divergent node, anchored to node-id + file:line,\n  direction stated (code-only path · flow-only node · behavioral\n  drift). Statuses set per node via set-status --unit.\n\n## Planning mode (drawing for future work)\n\nEvery planned node carries: status planned · refs to specs that ALREADY\nexist (a missing spec is flagged, never invented) · description =\nexpected behavior + 1–2 acceptance hints (\"emits WO.created; idempotent\non retry\") · priority relative to the critical path. Conditions name\ntheir planned predicate source; subprocess children are created\nup-front as skeletal planned pebbles. Implementation later maps each\nnode 1:1 to code+test (implementation-standard applies).\n\n## Two more modes\n\n- DIFF two workflow versions: align nodes by label+refs (never by\n  position); report added / removed / rewired / re-statused as a change\n  list; flag conditions or joins whose semantics changed silently.\n- IMPACT of changing a node: walk the node's refs + the code map\n  (importers/callers) outward; list downstream nodes, sibling workflows\n  sharing the subprocess, and apis needing re-verification — a\n  blast-radius checklist.",
    "kind": "text"
  },
